PROPOSAL - NOT APPLIED, NOT BUILT. For review before any kext work.
One user-client connection carries many CPU mappings (NVRM kext + nvrm_xnu).

WHY
  Every CPU mapping a process makes today costs one IOKit user-client connection for as long as the mapping
  lives. nvRmApiMapMemory does open(node) -> RM_MAP_MEMORY(ctl fd, memFd) -> mmap(memFd) -> close(memFd), and
  under IOKit the "fd" is a connection: the kext keeps ONE mapping context per client (NVRMUserClient::fMapList;
  a second RM_MAP_MEMORY on the same client finds the node in place and RM refuses it with NV_ERR_STATE_IN_USE),
  and clientMemoryForType maps whatever node is at the head. The context is never consumed, so a connection
  with a live mapping can never take another one, and nvrm_xnu_close parks it until its mapping goes.
  Measured: Cyberpunk held ~990 command-chunk mappings (so ~990 connections) before NVK slabs; with slabs the
  whole process holds 275 connections / 272 mappings. Every buffer the app maps is still one connection, and
  each open/close crosses the accelerator's arbitration lock (the WindowServer watchdog stall noted in
  nvrm_xnu.c). Linux does not have this limit because nv_mmap CONSUMES the context.

THE CHANGE (two parts, gated so either side can ship first)
  Kext (kexts/NVRM/NVRM.cpp):
   K1. clientMemoryForType: after the descriptor is built from the snapshot, consume the node it was built from -
       under fMapLock, and only if fMapList is still that node in the same fMapGen: pop it, free it with
       nvMapListFree on a one-element list, clear fMapLen/fMapLenNode, ++fMapGen. A mapping already made does not
       need the node (the descriptor holds its own copy of the ranges: withPhysicalAddress / withOptions copy them,
       nv_pm_ram_submd takes start+size).
   K2. New selector 7 sCaps -> scalar out bit0 = "mapping context is consumed at map time". The dispatch table
       grows by one entry; selectors 0..6 unchanged.
   K3. sBindFd's stale-context clear stays as is (it now almost never finds one).
  Userspace (nvkmd/nvrm/nvrm_xnu.c, nvRmApi.c):
   U1. On first use, ask selector 7 on a fresh connection. Without bit0 (old kext) nothing changes.
   U2. With bit0: keep a small set of hub connections per node type (default 4, NVRM_XNU_HUBS=n, 0 = off), each
       with one permanently bound fd and a mutex. nvrm_map_memory takes a hub's mutex across RM_MAP_MEMORY + mmap
       (another thread's RM_MAP_MEMORY on the same hub between the two would replace the context), uses the hub
       fd as memFd, and does not close it. If mmap fails after RM_MAP_MEMORY succeeded, issue RM_UNMAP_MEMORY
       and re-bind the hub fd (sBindFd clears the stale context) before releasing the mutex.
   U3. nvrm_xnu_munmap already unmaps by address (IOConnectUnmapMemory64(conn, 0, task, addr)); a hub is never
       closed or pooled while the process lives, so close/maps_on/pending_close never see hub connections.
   U4. Census: nvrm_xnu_live() already reports connections and mappings; add hub hits.

SKETCH OF K1 (illustrative; anchors from NVRM.cpp as of agent-cpuhot 97931d7)
--- a/kexts/NVRM/NVRM.cpp
+++ b/kexts/NVRM/NVRM.cpp
@@ IOReturn NVRMUserClient::clientMemoryForType(UInt32 type, IOOptionBits *options, IOMemoryDescriptor **memory)
     nv_alloc_mapping_context_t snapshot;
     NvU64 snapLen = 0;
     MemoryRange *ranges = NULL;
+    nv_alloc_mapping_list_node_t *snapNode = NULL; NvU64 snapGen = 0;
     IOLockLock(fMapLock);
@@
     snapshot = fMapList->context;
+    snapNode = fMapList; snapGen = fMapGen;
@@
     if (!md) return kIOReturnNoMemory;
+    // Consume the context this mapping was made from, as Linux nv_mmap does, so the next RM_MAP_MEMORY on this
+    // client is accepted. Only the node we copied, and only if nothing replaced it meanwhile.
+    IOLockLock(fMapLock);
+    if (fMapList == snapNode && fMapGen == snapGen) {
+        nv_alloc_mapping_list_node_t *one = fMapList; fMapList = one->pNext; one->pNext = NULL;
+        nvMapListFree(&one);
+        fMapLen = 0; fMapLenNode = nullptr; fMapGen++;
+    }
+    IOLockUnlock(fMapLock);
     if (caching == NV_MEMORY_UNCACHED) *options |= kIOMapInhibitCache;

RISKS (each needs a check or an arm before install)
  R1. Unmap bookkeeping. sBindFd's comment records that clearing the context on DETACH left live mappings
      unreleasable (VRAM filled and stayed full after exit). It must be shown that RM_UNMAP_MEMORY (issued on the
      ctl fd with pLinearAddress) and rm_cleanup_file_private do not need the per-file node after the map. Test:
      map/unmap 10,000 sysmem and vidmem buffers through one hub, then exit; RM's FB free must return to baseline,
      on the ctl and the device node.
  R2. Timing of the consume. If IOKit calls clientMemoryForType more than once per IOConnectMapMemory64, or a
      failed createMapping after a successful clientMemoryForType loses the context, the second mapping would
      find nothing. Check IOUserClient::mapClientMemory64 on this macOS (it calls clientMemoryForType once, then
      createMappingInTask); on failure userspace re-issues RM_MAP_MEMORY (U2).
  R3. Hub serialization. RM_MAP_MEMORY + mmap under one mutex per hub; with 4 hubs and nine render threads this
      must not become the new hot lock. Measure the hold time (two kernel calls) under the game.
  R4. Old/new mixing. New kext + old userspace: one map per connection, the node is consumed after it - harmless,
      but the "stale context" log in sBindFd goes quiet (expected). Old kext + new userspace: selector 7 is
      kIOReturnBadArgument -> hubs off. Both orders must be tested.
  R5. fMapLen / fMapLenNode / fMapGen. The length RM validated is bound to one node; consuming must reset it, or a
      later node at the same address could inherit a length (the existing generation logic exists for exactly
      that). nv_xnu_note_map_len must still find its node: it runs inside RM_MAP_MEMORY, before the map.
  R6. Ctl node path (sysmem through nv_xnu_alloc_md + snapLen): same consume; the "no validated length - refused"
      path must still refuse rather than map a consumed node.
  R7. Lifetime of hub connections. They are never pooled or closed until exit, so their RM state lives for the
      process; rm_cleanup_file_private at exit must free everything mapped through them (R1 test covers it).
  R8. Sandbox and WindowServer. Hubs are the same NVAccel-vended connection type, so the sandbox rule is unchanged,
      but WindowServer must still pass the gate: HEALTHY + steady pid + no new WindowServer .ips, and its own
      connection count should fall (census line).
  R9. Install path: kext/AuxKC = guarded install + dead-man, ONE component, reboot, WindowServer gate; never
      inside an IOP arm window. Userspace part ships with NVK and stays off against an old kext (U1).
